From sdavis2 at mail.nih.gov  Fri Dec 10 13:50:26 2004
From: sdavis2 at mail.nih.gov (Sean Davis)
Date: Fri Dec 10 13:50:49 2004
Subject: [BioC] Limma FDR question
In-Reply-To: <20041210122104.64632.qmail@web51706.mail.yahoo.com>
References: <20041210122104.64632.qmail@web51706.mail.yahoo.com>
Message-ID: <103D39F4-4AAA-11D9-8D09-000D933565E8@mail.nih.gov>

See ?p.adjust for specifics.  However, in a particular case, if one has 
100 genes with fdr less than 0.05, then 5 out of that 100 are expected 
to be false positive.  In other words, for any given fdr cutoff x, if 
there are N genes with fdr<x, then x*N genes are expected to be false 
discoveries.

Sean

